 ## Overview
-Direct Gaussian Splatting (dGS) is a method for N-dimensional splatting that replaces the covariance-derived conditioning step of standard N-DGS with explicit per-primitive parameters, removing the need for per-primitive matrix inversions and regression products during rendering [S1, unofficial]. It solves the computational bottleneck of recovering conditional mean, opacity, and covariance from a joint distribution by directly parameterizing the opacity precision and position regression operator [S1, unofficial]. Unlike N-DGS, which couples position and opacity through shared covariance blocks requiring expensive linear algebra, dGS separates these effects using a Cholesky precision factor for opacity and a factorized query-to-position regression operator with learnable per-dimension coupling [S1, unofficial]. It differs from sibling methods like [3D Student's t Distribution Splatting](4352-3d-student-s-t-distribution-splatting.md), [Generalized Exponential Splatting](4684-generalized-exponential-splatting.md), and [2D Gaussian Splatting](6068-2d-gaussian-splatting.md) by explicitly parameterizing the conditioning step rather than deriving it from a joint covariance matrix.
