 ### Direct Beta Splatting
-Direct Beta Splatting (dBS) is a variant of dGS that swaps the Gaussian opacity decay for a Beta opacity decay, following the UBS implementation. It reuses the direct conditional structure ($\mathbf{L}$, $\mathbf{V}_{pq}$, $\mathbf{V}_{qq}$) but derives coupling from bandwidth. Unlike dGS, which uses a separate learned coupling $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}$ for position, dBS derives the coupling $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_\beta$ from the opacity bandwidth $\boldsymbol{\beta}$. It is chosen when a Beta kernel is preferred for its specific decay properties, while retaining the computational benefits of direct conditioning.
+Direct Beta Splatting (dBS) is a variant of dGS that swaps the Gaussian opacity decay for a Beta opacity decay, following the UBS implementation [S1, unofficial]. It reuses the direct conditional structure ($\mathbf{L}$, $\mathbf{V}_{pq}$, $\mathbf{V}_{qq}$) but derives coupling from bandwidth [S1, unofficial]. Unlike dGS, which uses a separate learned coupling $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}$ for position, dBS derives the coupling $\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_\beta$ from the opacity bandwidth $\boldsymbol{\beta}$ [S1, unofficial]. It is chosen when a Beta kernel is preferred for its specific decay properties, while retaining the computational benefits of direct conditioning [S1, unofficial].
 
